<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">+&nbsp;Crit</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">–&nbsp;hide&nbsp;block</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<blockquote>
<p>The SCP is a humanoid entity similar to SCP-096, being tall, disproportionate, and skinny</p>
</blockquote>
<p>If you're describing your anomaly as similar to another one, especially one so popular, it's likely to feel overly similar to your readers as well.</p>
<p>SCPs that are scary monsters and not much else have a hard time on the site these days, since they were done so much back in series 1 a decade ago. Generally successful articles nowadays tell a story, using the anomaly as a vessel to tell it. While this does have a vague narrative in here, it has a few glaring issues:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>The go crazy a few hours after seeing the image or video, and kill any living thing they see, but can't die themselves.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Things that make you go crazy and things which induce a 'rage state' are two of the most overdone and generally unpopular tropes on the site and would really struggle to survive without bringing something completely new to the table.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>it's an endless maze of houses and shops, some buildings being made of obscure materials, including glass, wires, and human components</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Additionally, endless dimensions have been done quite a few times before, most notably in <a href="/scp-3008">SCP-3008</a>, which survives more from its journal than from the actual anomaly.</p>
<p>Overall, this has the problem that both components of it have been done in several other articles, and generally done better. The lack of any story beyond it having killed a village (something which isn't very exciting for a reader, as there's nothing special about reading a clinical description stating that some people died), doesn't help to alleviate these issues. I'd probably recommend considering other ideas here, and will link some guides that might help you in the future. I also recommend you read some more recent articles from <a href="/scp-series-5">Series 5</a> and <a href="/scp-series-6">Series 6</a> to get an idea of what works on the site now.</p>
</div>
</div>
